\f0\fs22 \cf2 On the morning the cure came up the line, Jamison Hardy was in a meeting he was enjoying, which was rare enough that he had made a note of it in his own hand.\
The item under discussion was the protection of culturally significant landmarks, a campaign old money had been funding since before Hardy was old enough to be taken to the meetings, on the sound principle that if you call your own harbour frontage a landmark for long enough, some minister will eventually agree that the public has a duty to protect it. The landmarks in question were, without exception, houses belonging to the people around the table. The subcommittee had that morning secured a further appropriation to protect the beaches south to Coogee, on the grounds of their inestimable cultural value to a nation most of whom would never be admitted to them.\
Nothing whatsoever was being built. Nothing was being cleaned, dredged, planted or repaired. The money was going out beautifully and coming to nothing at all, and Hardy, who had a connoisseur\u8217 ?s appreciation for expenditure that left no evidence behind, was in the finest mood he had enjoyed all quarter.\
Then the presenter from the labs was shown in.\
He was a young man in a good suit that his mother had probably chosen, and he had rehearsed. He had a two-page summary, and he got through eleven words of it before the room started interrupting him with congratulations. Every disease. Not the treatment of every disease, not the management of it; the end of it. Bones that mended, organs that repaired themselves, a lifespan nobody at the labs had yet been game to put a number on. The young man said the words \u8220 ?no longer necessary\u8221 ? three times about three different industries and did not once notice himself saying them.\
The board was delighted. Two of the directors applauded. Someone said it was the finest thing the company had ever done, and meant it, and would be dead within the year of something entirely ordinary.\
Hardy went quiet.\
He was the only one in the room who owned anything. The others held positions, portfolios, options and expectations, all of which are things you hold because somebody else owns the thing behind them, and none of which had ever taught a man to do the arithmetic Hardy had been doing since he was fourteen. Hardy owned the hospitals. He owned the insurers who paid the hospitals, and the funds that owned the insurers, and a respectable slice of the pharmaceutical concerns that sold to both, and the debt of the people who could not afford any of it. He had not built that; he had inherited most of it and married a little more, and he had spent his working life keeping it in careful equilibrium, the way a man keeps a very old wall standing by never once leaning on it.\
He thanked the young man warmly, and then, because he was a courteous man and the young man had clearly worked hard, he thanked him again.\
\u8220 ?Don\u8217 ?t copy the file,\u8221 ? he said. \u8220 ?Not for the board. Not for me. Leave it where it is.\u8221 ?\
The young man said of course, sir, and glowed all the way to the lift, believing he had been trusted.\
Hardy sat through the rest of the agenda and voted correctly on all of it.\
He spent the afternoon deciding who to ask, and every name he considered was wrong.\
Scientists would say yes. That was not a criticism; it was their function, and Hardy had never once resented a man for doing the thing he was paid to do. Doctors would say yes, and would say it publicly, and would say it in a way that made anyone who disagreed sound like a murderer. Ministers would leak, not from conviction but from the ordinary human need to be the first at a dinner party to know something. The board would want a briefing, and a briefing was a document with names on it, and the names would be the names of the men in that morning\u8217 ?s meeting, and every one of them had a wife.\
What Hardy wanted was an answer he could hold up. He wanted it produced by people with no stake in the outcome, no standing to be quoted, and no idea what they were looking at.\
So he took the finance department, and from the finance department he took the juniors.\
There were twelve of them: accountants, economists, three finance advisers and a woman from Treasury Modelling whose job title ran to fourteen words and described, in essence, the making of graphs. They were the people who spent their days building models nobody read, of futures nobody intended to have, for reports that existed so that a decision already taken could be described afterwards as evidence-based. They were good at it. They were good at it the way a well-made hinge is good: silently, and without any expectation of being looked at.\
They were told they had been selected. The word was chosen with some care, and it worked exactly as intended. Four of them assumed it was a promotion. Two rang their mothers. One, a young man named\u8212 ?but that is the whole point, and we shall come to it\u8212 ?one of them brought along a folder of proposals he had been carrying for four years, waiting for a senior person to be trapped in a room with him.\
They were collected in the dark, in three cars, and not told where they were going.\
The site was in the Pale, though nobody called it that then; it was simply the part of the city where the labs were, because land there was cheap and neighbours were not the sort who wrote letters. The lift went down for a long time. It went down long enough that two of the juniors got the giggles, and then stopped, the way people stop when a lift keeps going.\
Fifty metres, and then the pressure doors, which sighed. Then a corridor.\
The corridor was where Hardy stopped and read a sign. He stood in front of it in a suit worth more than the annual maintenance budget of the level he was standing on, in a building he owned outright, and he read the sign twice and then took the left-hand branch, and one of the accountants behind him noticed, and filed it away, and mentioned it to nobody for the rest of his life, which was that day.\
Somebody took a photograph of the corridor and was asked not to.\
The room was a conference room. Bad light, a whiteboard nobody had cleaned properly, a jug of water and twelve chairs, which meant that Hardy stood.\
\u8220 ?I don\u8217 ?t know any of your names,\u8221 ? he said, \u8220 ?and I have declined to be told them. Please don\u8217 ?t tell me. You have been brought here precisely because I don\u8217 ?t know you.\u8221 ?\
They took this as a security formality, which is the most sensible thing to take it as.\
He gave them the question. What happens to the economy if disease ends. Not slows, not becomes manageable: ends. He gave them access to a data feed that would answer any question they cared to put to it and would not permit anything to leave the room. He gave them seven hours.\
He did not tell them why seven. One of them asked, in the reasonable manner of a person hoping to be granted eight, and Hardy said, \u8220 ?Operational,\u8221 ? and the young man wrote operational in his notebook and underlined it.\
Then came the questions from the floor, and there were nine of them, and every one was professional, and every one was beside the point.\
Was this a memo or a deck. Was there a template. Were they assuming current tax settings, or should they model reform. Was the audience the board or the regulator, because those were different documents. Should they present a base case only, or a base case with sensitivities. Would there be a version-control convention. What was the citation standard. What happened if they needed longer.\
And then, from the back, the ninth question, which was about lunch.\
Hardy brightened. He had thought about lunch that morning for a good deal longer than he had thought about anything else, and had settled it personally, and it pleased him enormously to be able to say so. There was a catering order. It was a very good one. There would be a hot service at one and something lighter later, and the coffee was the coffee from the executive floor and not the coffee from anywhere else, and he had checked.\
They were delighted. Several of them said so.\
He wished them well and left them to it, and the door closed, and it was the last time that door opened while anybody in the room was in a position to notice.\
Upstairs, in a site manager\u8217 ?s office with a desk built for a man half Hardy\u8217 ?s tailoring, he spread out the drawings for the dome and got to work.\
He was extremely good at this. Whatever else may be said about Jamison Hardy, and there is a great deal, he could cost a thing. He worked in cubic metres. Ultra-high-performance concrete at ten metres over the whole footprint, plus the shafts, plus the cap on the lift head, plus the standing charge on the plant, plus the premium for continuous pour, because a cold joint is a seam and a seam is a thing that can be opened. He did the sums twice, once at the contract rate and once at the rate he intended to pay, and the difference between them was, by itself, more money than everyone downstairs would earn between them in their entire lives.\
Outside and four levels below him, the contractors had been on site since the previous afternoon. They had been engaged eleven days earlier. Hardy had signed the engagement himself, on the same day the labs had first flagged, in a routine progress note buried in a monthly, that they were seeing something interesting on the platform.\
At a little after three, the site manager put his head in and said, with a diffidence that suggested he had been arguing with himself in the corridor, that Professor Hermann was upstairs and asking whether he might have five minutes.\
Hardy said of course.\
Hamish Hermann came in the way he came into everything, which was slightly too fast, with a printout in his hand and his reading glasses pushed up into his hair and forgotten there.\
\u8220 ?Jamison,\u8221 ? he said. \u8220 ?All this way. You\u8217 ?ve heard, obviously. You\u8217 ?ve heard.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?ve heard.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Sit down. No\u8212 ?you sit down, it\u8217 ?s your building.\u8221 ? Hermann laughed at his own joke, which was a habit of thirty years, and sat, and put the printout on the desk and then didn\u8217 ?t look at it once, because he did not need it; he knew all of it.\
He talked for eleven minutes. He talked about scale-up, and about whether the platform would hold at volume, and about the two ways it might not, and how they would know inside a fortnight which. He talked about a bigger trial, and about the ethics committee, and about the fact that the ethics committee was going to be the easiest conversation of his career for the first time in his career. He said that the real question was not whether they could afford to give it away but whether they could afford not to, and that he had been turning that sentence over for three days and could not make it come out any other way.\
He talked about children.\
He said, at one point, that Janine had not been home properly in a month, that she had all but moved into the annexe, that he had told her twice to go home and she had not, and that she had a daughter, and that the daughter was not well, and that he had never in his life seen anybody work like that.\
Hardy listened to all of it. He was, by every account that survives, a good listener, and it was not a technique.\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s magnificent,\u8221 ? he said, and he believed that. \u8220 ?Keep going.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?You\u8217 ?ll have my scale-up note by Friday.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Good.\u8221 ?\
Hardy stood and put his hand out, and Hermann took it, and Hardy said nothing whatever that was untrue, and Hermann went back downstairs.\
The report came in at seven hours and four minutes, which was late, and the covering note apologised for it.\
It was competent. That is the whole of the horror, and it does not improve on inspection. It had an executive summary of a page and a half. It had an assumptions page, properly caveated. It had three scenarios, named Base, Adverse and Severe, with the sensitivities set out in a table that had clearly been fought over. It had a risk register. It had an appendix, running to sixty pages, that no living person has ever read.\
Nobody in that room wrote a word of it in anger. They arrived at it. They checked it. They were quietly pleased with it, in the way you are pleased with a piece of work that has come out clean, and two of them had already begun, privately, to think of it as the best thing they had ever done, which it was.\
The recommendation was one sentence.\
The miracle drug must be destroyed, all research must be destroyed, and all related research staff must be eliminated.\
At the door, with his bag over his shoulder, in the middle of asking somebody about the cars home, one of them stopped walking.\
He had done the arithmetic on all related research staff.\
He said so, out loud, in a small voice, and there was a pause, and then a woman from Treasury Modelling told him kindly not to be like that, and somebody else said it was a very long day and everybody was tired, and the young man went red to the ears and apologised, and was embarrassed for the rest of the time available to him, which was not long.\
That is the last thing that happened in that room in this book.\
Hardy read the recommendation upstairs, alone, and what he felt was relief.\
Here is the mechanism, and it is worth stating plainly because he never once stated it to himself.\
He had asked a question he already knew the answer to. The answer had now come back to him from outside himself, in writing, on company letterhead, from twelve people whose names he did not know and had refused to be told. That made it not an opinion. That made it a finding. And a man does not defy a finding merely because it is inconvenient to his feelings; a man complies with it, at cost to himself, and says nothing, and lets history make of him what it will.\
He carried that for the rest of his life and it never cracked once.\
What happened next was procurement.\
The recommendation said destroy, so destroy was costed, and it was costed properly, because that is what the department was for. Incineration of a facility at that depth is not a fire; it is a sustained thermal event requiring oxygen feed, containment, air handling and a certification regime, and it came in at four times the alternative. The alternative was concrete. Concrete was cheaper.\
And\u8212 ?this is the part that matters, and nobody in the chain noticed it, and it has cost the world rather more than the four times\u8217 ? difference ever would have\u8212 ?concrete produced a receipt.\
A destroyed thing has to be certified destroyed by somebody who went down and looked, and that somebody has a name, and that name goes on a form, and forms are kept. A sealed thing is certified by an invoice. Sixty-one thousand cubic metres, delivered, poured, signed for by a foreman on a docket, filed by a clerk who thought it was footings.\
The instruction went out as one line in a board paper, executed inside the week across a dozen installations by people who never met Hardy and never asked. Nobody was evacuated, because nobody was told there was anything to evacuate from, and the last shift went down at the usual hour.\
And that is how both of them survived. Intact, catalogued, dry, fifty metres down under ten metres of dome, sealed in the most conscientiously engineered dark on the continent: the cure, and the other thing, the one the platform had been built for in the first place. Preserved perfectly. Waiting a couple of hundred years for a man with a drill and a deadline.\
Jamison Hardy destroyed the cure for every human disease by keeping it in mint condition, and neither he nor anybody else ever noticed.\
He went up in the car a little after eleven.\
He was, by then, quite certain what he was. He had saved the entire world that day, and had done it alone, and had done it at a cost he would carry by himself, and no one would ever know. That was the curse of it. That was, he thought, looking out at the lights of a city that had no idea, the curse of the true hero: to be owed everything by everybody, forever, and to be thanked by nobody, ever.\
He was a little tearful about it, in the car, on the way up.\
He was right, of course. Nobody ever found out. Nobody ever will. Two centuries later a man will recite that report\u8217 ?s finding at a much better lunch, as his own opinion, having never in his life heard of a single person who was in that room; and another man will shrug and say, apparently not, and go on with his day. There is no file. There is no whisper. There is one docket for sixty-one thousand cubic metres of concrete, in a box, in a storage facility that has changed hands nine times.\
He was thanked by nobody, ever, and he was owed everything, forever, and he was correct on both counts, and we are the only ones who will ever be in a position to tell him so.}
